From: mscharged-port
Subject: Append NET MD5's bit length in little-endian bytes natively
Upstream-base: a353c8f2dfb34eb08fecd725965a46ea629428e3
Classification: native byte-order compatibility

NETMD5GetDigest appends the message's bit length as NETSwapBytes32 values
stored as u32 words. On the Wii that leaves little-endian bytes, as MD5
requires; on a little-endian host it leaves them reversed, and
ProcessBlock, which already reads words through the native
NETReadSwappedBytes32, saw a byte-swapped length. Every digest of a
non-empty message was wrong, including the HMAC that signs the game's
ranking submissions (NetworkStats).

Natively the length words are written with NETWriteSwappedBytes32, the
store ProcessBlock pairs with; on the Wii that is the same stwbrx store.
Checked against a reference MD5 for lengths 0 to 1000.

diff --git a/src/RVL_SDK/net/md5.c b/src/RVL_SDK/net/md5.c
index 7758d43..0b352f0 100644
--- a/src/RVL_SDK/net/md5.c
+++ b/src/RVL_SDK/net/md5.c
@@ -209,8 +209,15 @@ void NETMD5GetDigest(NETMD5Context* context, void* digest)
         memset(&context->buffer8[buffer_index], 0, buffer_space - 8);
     }
 
+#if defined(MSCHARGED_NATIVE)
+    // MD5 appends the bit length as little-endian words; store their bytes
+    // as the swapped Wii stores do, which ProcessBlock reads back.
+    NETWriteSwappedBytes32(&context->buffer32[14], (u32)total_length);
+    NETWriteSwappedBytes32(&context->buffer32[15], (u32)(total_length >> 32));
+#else
     context->buffer32[14] = NETSwapBytes32((u32)total_length);
     context->buffer32[15] = NETSwapBytes32((u32)(total_length >> 32));
+#endif
     ProcessBlock(context);
 
     NETWriteSwappedBytes32(p, context->state[0]);
